WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.610
Students say the filter sees a door.

2
00:00:03.290 --> 00:00:04.830
Make them point at the

3
00:00:04.830 --> 00:00:06.310
nine multiplications.

4
00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:08.720
The door is their name for

5
00:00:08.720 --> 00:00:09.900
a pattern of edges.

6
00:00:11.240 --> 00:00:13.880
In the seminar: a grid, nine

7
00:00:13.880 --> 00:00:16.000
multiplications, and a stop

8
00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:18.000
before any photo of a person.

9
00:00:19.210 --> 00:00:21.470
A grey image is one grid.

10
00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:25.260
A colour image is three, and each cell

11
00:00:25.260 --> 00:00:27.560
is a brightness, not an object.

12
00:00:28.900 --> 00:00:31.000
A three by three filter lays on the

13
00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:33.260
picture and multiplies nine cells.

14
00:00:33.940 --> 00:00:36.280
It writes that sum as the new pixel.

15
00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:39.940
An upright-edge filter lights walls.

16
00:00:40.760 --> 00:00:43.600
A blur averages neighbours, and neither

17
00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:45.280
knows it is looking at a face.

18
00:00:46.630 --> 00:00:48.770
Here is one position of the filter.

19
00:00:49.480 --> 00:00:51.940
Nine multiplications become one

20
00:00:51.940 --> 00:00:53.800
number, and the name of the

21
00:00:53.800 --> 00:00:55.800
object is not in that number.

22
00:00:57.080 --> 00:00:57.560
Quick check!

23
00:00:58.200 --> 00:01:00.020
A grey picture on a

24
00:01:00.020 --> 00:01:01.860
computer is which of these?

25
00:01:03.080 --> 00:01:04.080
A grid of numbers.

26
00:01:04.920 --> 00:01:07.720
Names, if they appear, come from many

27
00:01:07.720 --> 00:01:09.680
layers, not from one filter.

28
00:01:11.060 --> 00:01:13.440
Pairs say the nine-term sum out

29
00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:15.600
loud once before they run the

30
00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:16.740
filter on the whole picture.

31
00:01:17.380 --> 00:01:18.840
Drawn edges are enough.

32
00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:20.920
Your turn.

33
00:01:21.540 --> 00:01:24.060
Does a three by three filter already

34
00:01:24.060 --> 00:01:25.680
know it is looking at a face?

35
00:01:26.990 --> 00:01:27.810
Remember this.

36
00:01:28.430 --> 00:01:30.210
A filter mixes neighbours.

37
00:01:31.070 --> 00:01:33.030
It does not name what it sees.

38
00:01:34.350 --> 00:01:35.670
Try it in your next lecture.

39
00:01:36.350 --> 00:01:38.750
Collect the sentence it knows it

40
00:01:38.750 --> 00:01:41.190
is a door, and answer it with the

41
00:01:41.190 --> 00:01:42.410
nine multiplications.
